WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:06.602
Recipes, workflows and layer rules can all run JavaScript, and the Scripting module runs it.

00:00:06.602 --> 00:00:11.004
This video shows where scripts run, which methods they can call, how to

00:00:11.004 --> 00:00:14.320
add your own, and how to limit what a script can do.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:21.948
A script is a prefix, a colon and the code.

00:00:21.948 --> 00:00:27.632
The js prefix selects the JavaScript engine, which uses Jint, a JavaScript interpreter

00:00:27.632 --> 00:00:32.390
for .NET, and the file prefix selects an engine that reads a text file.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:35.170
The Scripting module is always enabled.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.027
Scripts run wherever Orchard Core takes an expression:

00:00:39.027 --> 00:00:42.289
in any text value of a recipe, in the Script Task and the

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:46.484
expressions of a workflow, in the Javascript condition of a layer rule, and

00:00:46.484 --> 00:00:48.400
in the external login settings.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:58.311
In a recipe, any text value can be a script between square brackets, with its prefix.

00:00:58.311 --> 00:01:02.578
Orchard Core replaces it with the result before the step runs.

00:01:02.578 --> 00:01:07.410
Here uuid generates an identifier, and the file prefix loads a snippet.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:12.816
Variables declared at the top are computed on first read, then reused.

00:01:12.816 --> 00:01:15.451
Three methods exist only in a recipe:

00:01:15.451 --> 00:01:20.720
variables, parameters, which reads a value of the recipe environment such as the

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.560
admin user id, and configuration, which reads a tenant configuration key.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:34.656
In a workflow, the Script Task runs a whole script, and expression fields

00:01:34.656 --> 00:01:36.842
can use the JavaScript syntax.

00:01:36.842 --> 00:01:39.878
Let's open a sample workflow that does both:

00:01:39.878 --> 00:01:40.667
Sample:

00:01:40.667 --> 00:01:41.760
order total.

00:01:42.214 --> 00:01:45.531
The Script Task has Available Outcomes and a Script.

00:01:45.531 --> 00:01:50.906
This script reads the query string with queryString, stores values with setVariable, and

00:01:50.906 --> 00:01:55.194
ends with setOutcome, which chooses the outcome the workflow follows.

00:01:55.647 --> 00:02:00.357
A Set Variable Task takes its Value from an expression, and the syntax

00:02:00.357 --> 00:02:03.394
list offers Literal, Liquid and JavaScript.

00:02:03.394 --> 00:02:05.129
Here it is JavaScript.

00:02:05.129 --> 00:02:08.847
A separate video covers workflow expressions in depth.

00:02:09.309 --> 00:02:12.623
The Available data tab lists what an activity can use.

00:02:12.623 --> 00:02:16.489
Under Functions are the methods a script can call in a workflow.

00:02:20.689 --> 00:02:24.696
Modules register the methods a script can call.

00:02:24.696 --> 00:02:26.435
Always available:

00:02:26.435 --> 00:02:31.424
log, protect, encrypt, decrypt, base64, html, gzip and uuid.

00:02:31.424 --> 00:02:40.269
Contents adds methods for content items, Layers adds checks such as isAnonymous, and Queries adds executeQuery.

00:02:41.019 --> 00:02:44.437
Other methods exist for one evaluation only.

00:02:44.437 --> 00:02:52.435
A workflow gets workflow, input, output, property, variable and setVariable, and a Script Task adds setOutcome.

00:02:52.435 --> 00:02:57.699
Each of these is a scoped method, passed in when the evaluation starts.

00:03:01.899 --> 00:03:04.426
This is how the uuid method is written.

00:03:04.426 --> 00:03:10.602
A provider implements IGlobalMethodProvider and returns GlobalMethod objects, where Name is what scripts

00:03:10.602 --> 00:03:15.149
call and Method is a factory that receives the service provider and returns

00:03:15.149 --> 00:03:16.609
the delegate to run.

00:03:17.359 --> 00:03:21.775
Register the provider as a singleton in the Startup class of your module.

00:03:21.775 --> 00:03:26.135
The factory usually runs lazily, the first time a script reads the name,

00:03:26.135 --> 00:03:28.539
with the services of that evaluation.

00:03:29.289 --> 00:03:32.211
A GlobalMethod can also set AsyncMethod.

00:03:32.211 --> 00:03:37.230
Scripts then get a second function, with the same name followed by Async,

00:03:37.230 --> 00:03:39.073
that returns a promise.

00:03:39.073 --> 00:03:44.409
The Contents module registers newContentItem and newContentItemAsync this way.

00:03:48.609 --> 00:03:52.312
To run a script from your own code, call IScriptingManager.

00:03:52.312 --> 00:03:57.438
EvaluateAsync takes the directive, picks the engine from its prefix, runs the script

00:03:57.438 --> 00:04:00.742
with the registered methods, and releases the scope.

00:04:00.742 --> 00:04:03.989
A string with no known prefix comes back unchanged.

00:04:04.739 --> 00:04:08.711
To give a script data for one call, pass a scoped method provider.

00:04:08.711 --> 00:04:13.621
The Users module does this for its external login scripts, because prepared scripts

00:04:13.621 --> 00:04:17.703
are cached by their text, and data written into the script would add

00:04:17.703 --> 00:04:19.689
a cache entry for every login.

00:04:23.889 --> 00:04:27.587
By default a script has no time, statement or memory limit.

00:04:27.587 --> 00:04:32.706
If people other than administrators can write scripts, configure the options of Jint

00:04:32.706 --> 00:04:35.949
with MaxStatements, TimeoutInterval or LimitMemory.

00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:39.419
One options instance serves every engine of the tenant.

00:04:40.169 --> 00:04:43.993
In a workflow, a script that throws or returns the wrong type falls

00:04:43.993 --> 00:04:46.560
back to a default, and the error is logged.

00:04:46.560 --> 00:04:50.331
A script stopped by a limit faults the workflow, as does any error

00:04:50.331 --> 00:04:53.369
when Fault the workflow on script errors is checked.

00:04:54.119 --> 00:04:59.431
The engine guards against unbounded recursion by raising a JavaScript error that a

00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:02.450
script can catch, not by ending the process.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:07.219
Each tenant also reuses a small pool of engines, sized by EnginePoolSize.

00:05:09.219 --> 00:05:13.784
Scripts are strings with a prefix, evaluated by the Scripting module with the

00:05:13.784 --> 00:05:18.734
methods that modules register, and the Scripting page of the documentation lists the

00:05:18.734 --> 00:05:20.769
methods and the engine options.

00:05:20.769 --> 00:05:22.199
Thanks for watching.

